<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cajamarca</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cajamarca is a city in the northern highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, capital of the Cajamarca region. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> administrative centre with baths and temples, it was where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and his small Spanish force ambushed the Inca emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> on 16 November 1532, capturing him and killing many of his retinue. Atahualpa offered a huge ransom in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> but was executed there in 1533.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cajamarca.jpg" alt="Cajamarca" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SrDingleberry69 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catedral_de_Cajamarca_Per%C3%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cajamarca stands at about 2,750 m above sea level in an inter-Andean valley irrigated by three rivers, the Mashcon, the San Lucas and the Chonta; the first two join here to form the Cajamarca river. The climate is subtropical highland, pleasant by day and cold at night, with rain mostly from October to April. The soil is very fertile. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> name, Kashamarka, is usually explained as town or province of thorny plants, from kasha, thorn, and marka, town or region. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega">Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</a> proposed qasa, frost, instead, and that false etymology still appears in some dictionaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city and its surroundings have been occupied by several cultures for more than 2,000 years. Nearby sites such as Cumbe Mayo and Kuntur Wasi preserve traces of cultures older than Chavín. Huacaloma, a ceremonial centre where fire rituals were performed, dates from about 1500 to 1000 BCE. The Cajamarca culture, known mainly from fine ceramics of white kaolin clay fired above 1,000 degrees, kept an unbroken style from about 200-100 BCE until the Spanish conquest. Its pottery reached its widest distribution between 700 and 900, when it is found at many sites of the Wari Empire, as far south as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pikillacta">Pikillacta</a>.</p><p>Between 1463 and 1471 the generals Capac Yupanqui and his nephew Tupac Inca Yupanqui conquered Cajamarca for the Inca Empire, then ruled by Tupac Inca Yupanqui's father, Pachacutiq. The town had been founded by other peoples about a century earlier, around 1320. Colonial chroniclers tell of a Cuismancu kingdom that ruled the area before the Incas. The Incas rebuilt the city to their own canons, but little survives, since the Spanish also rebuilt it after the conquest. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> reached Cajamarca in 1532, he learned that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> was resting at the nearby hot springs of Pultumarca and sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> to invite him to a feast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cajamarca is the capital and largest city of the Cajamarca Region and a commercial centre of the northern Andes, known for its dairy products, notably the mantecoso cheese, and for mining nearby. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organization-of-american-states">Organization of American States</a> designated it a site of Historical and Cultural Heritage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> in 1986. Its six Spanish colonial churches were all built in the 17th century; the Cathedral took 80 years to build, from 1682 to 1762, and its facade remains unfinished. Baños del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>, the hot springs town nearby, is a popular visiting place. The city celebrates Carnival each year with parades and dances.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cajamarca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 16 November 1532, at <b>Cajamarca</b>, they ambushed Atahualpa’s escort, killed many of his attendants and took him prisoner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Incas expanded from Cusco in the mid-fifteenth century, their armies conquered <b>Cajamarca</b> and other peoples of the northern highlands and then turned against the coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Viceroyalty of Peru grew out of the conquest of the Inca Empire by Francisco Pizarro and his companions, who captured the Sapa Inca Atahualpa at <b>Cajamarca</b> in 1532 and entered Cuzco in 1533.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Huayna Capac died around 1527, his sons Huáscar and Atahualpa fought a civil war (1529–1532), and in November 1532 the Spaniards captured the victor, Atahualpa, at <b>Cajamarca</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Francisco Pizarro's force of fewer than 200 men ambushed Atahualpa's retinue in the square of <b>Cajamarca</b>, killing thousands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-16">16 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Chincha's wealth earned it a privileged position under the Incas: at <b>Cajamarca</b> in 1532, the lord of Chincha was the only lord besides Atahualpa carried in a litter, and he was killed in the attack.</blockquote><a href="/state/chincha-kingdom">Chincha Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
